(2654972, 368261, 20530, '<td valign="top">Dang! I wish I could think of ideas like this. Right on the 1/3s! What I really like about this image is the statement it makes. It''s one of those photographs where the title is essential for the image to make sense.<br/> <br/>I love this setup and think that with a few improvements you''d have a top-notch shot and should even consider a series of these. The square crop works very well, here. (Of course I''m a rare fan of square crops). Here are my thoughts; take them for what they''re worth:<br/> <br/>I can see a couple of spots where the base material is showing. I think that this could have been avoided by putting the sand in a pan or somehow getting a thicker layer. That might have also given you deeper grooves which would have given the image more depth. I think the "gold" is the key element of the image and has not quite been done justice. I''d be in favor of some softer light, sharper focus, a lower angle and possibly a tighter crop. You might also get some good results with different DOF. Regardless of my nitpicks, it''s quite interesting and gets high marks for creative thinking!</td>', 'Dang! I wish I could think of ideas like this. Right on the 1/3s! What I really like about this image is the statement it makes. It''s one of those photographs where the title is essential for the image to make sense. I love this setup and think that with a few improvements you''d have a top-notch shot and should even consider a series of these. The square crop works very well, here. (Of course I''m a rare fan of square crops). Here are my thoughts; take them for what they''re worth: I can see a couple of spots where the base material is showing. I think that this could have been avoided by putting the sand in a pan or somehow getting a thicker layer. That might have also given you deeper grooves which would have given the image more depth. I think the "gold" is the key element of the image and has not quite been done justice. I''d be in favor of some softer light, sharper focus, a lower angle and possibly a tighter crop. You might also get some good results with different DOF. Regardless of my nitpicks, it''s quite interesting and gets high marks for creative thinking!', '2006-07-24 13:22:56.000000', NULL, 1),
